    {
      "title": "India-US trade deal: Why talks are back on table amid fresh tariff threat",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/india-us-trade-deal-why-talks-are-back-on-table-amid-fresh-tariff-threat-126112700266_1.html",
      "description": "India and the US finalised the framework for the first phase of their bilateral trade agreement (BTA) in February this year, following negotiations that were launched in February 2025.\nBut changes in Washington's tariff structure have brought the two sides back to the negotiating table.\nThe US has proposed a fresh set of tariffs on 14 economies, including India. Here is an explainer to understand the developments so far.\nWHY THIS TRADE PACT?\n---------------------------\nIndia is pursuing the agreement to safeguard the competitiveness of its exports amid high American tariffs and secure an edge over rival South and South East Asian manufacturing economies.\nWHY THESE TARIFFS?\n------------------------\nThe US is using tariffs against its trading partners such as India to address trade deficits, shield domestic industries from foreign competition and press countries to negotiate trade agreements on terms favourable to Washington.\nThe US has alleged that trading partners like India a",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-27T05:20:40"
    },
    {
      "title": "India-US trade deal close to completion: Goyal amid fresh tariff threat",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/india-us-trade-deal-nears-completion-piyush-goyal-amid-fresh-tariff-threat-126112700246_1.html",
      "description": "Commerce and Industry Minister Piyush Goyal on Friday said the India-US trade deal is nearing completion, with the process and roadmap for finalising the pact, which had earlier held up negotiations, now taking shape.\n\"The US trade deal is close to culmination. The process and pathway, which was always something which held back finalising the deal, is now taking some shape,\" Goyal said while addressing the India Debt Capital Market Summit 2026 virtually.\nThe remarks came after the latest US Trade Representative's (USTR) report proposed 25 per cent tariffs on imports from India, with a provision to cap them at 17 per cent if the two countries agree to a bilateral trade deal, giving it a slight edge over Asian competitors.",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-27T05:04:02"
    }
  ]
